use ../../../lib/UiComponent

from ../../../behaviors/Positioning import Positioning
from ../../../behaviors/Presence import Presence

export ui-component Tooltip:
    description:
        A tooltip is a short piece of text that appears next to something when
        the user points at it or focuses it, such as "Bold (Ctrl+B)" beside an
        icon button. It goes away when they move on. It never takes focus.

    behaviors:
        - @{Positioning}
        - @{Presence}

    showing:
        It appears when the pointer rests on the thing it belongs to for a
        moment, or straight away when that thing gets keyboard focus. Once one
        tooltip has appeared, moving to a neighbouring one shows it straight
        away, without waiting again. The component can change how long it
        waits. Whether it appears on touch screens is up to the chosen library.

    hiding:
        It goes after a short pause when the pointer leaves, and straight away
        when focus leaves, when the user presses Escape, or when they click or
        press on the thing it belongs to. Escape closes only the tooltip,
        leaving anything underneath it open. Whether scrolling the page hides
        it is up to the chosen library. Only one tooltip shows at a time.

    hoverable:
        The pointer can move from the thing onto the tooltip without it
        disappearing, so the user can read it, zoom in on it, or select its
        text. It stays until the pointer leaves both.

    reading:
        Its text is linked to the thing it belongs to, so assistive tools read
        it out when that thing is focused, whether the tooltip is showing or
        not. It usually adds a description. For an IconButton, it can repeat
        the button's name instead.

    triggers:
        Several things can share one tooltip, such as a row of toolbar
        buttons. It attaches to whichever one the user is on, and moving
        between them moves it without hiding it.

    content:
        Short, plain text. It may show a small arrow pointing at the thing it
        belongs to, which follows where Positioning actually put it.

    disabled:
        The component can turn a tooltip off. Things that are disabled can
        still show a tooltip, so the user can find out why they're disabled,
        as long as the chosen library lets them be pointed at or focused.

    nonGoals:
        - Nothing in it can be pressed or focused. Links or buttons in a panel
            that appears on hover make it a HoverCard, and anything else a
            Popover
        - It is never the only place important information lives. Touch
            screen users may never see it
        - It isn't a label. Something that needs a name has one of its own
